# Windows 11 + PowerShell Environment

The user runs Windows 11 with PowerShell as their default shell.

## Shell Commands

- Use PowerShell syntax, not bash/Unix.
- Use `\` for path separators (or `/` which PowerShell also accepts).
- Use PowerShell cmdlets and operators where appropriate (e.g., `Remove-Item` over `rm`, `Get-Content` over `cat`).

## Git Commits in PowerShell

Bash heredocs (`<<'EOF'`) do NOT work. Use PowerShell here-strings for multiline commit messages:

```powershell
$msg = @"
feat(scope): subject line here

Body paragraph here without double quotes inside.
Use single quotes or rephrase to avoid them.
"@
git commit -m $msg
```

**Important:** Avoid double quotes (`"`) inside the message body — git on Windows splits arguments on them. Rephrase or use single quotes instead.

## Common Differences

| Bash | PowerShell |
|------|------------|
| `export VAR=value` | `$env:VAR = "value"` |
| `source .venv/bin/activate` | `.venv\Scripts\Activate.ps1` |
| `rm -rf dir` | `Remove-Item -Recurse -Force dir` |
| `cat file` | `Get-Content file` |
| `echo $VAR` | `$env:VAR` or `Write-Output $var` |
| `command1 && command2` | `command1; if ($LASTEXITCODE -eq 0) { command2 }` |
| `which command` | `Get-Command command` |
